WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.947
A friend opens a small café, and you help her set up.

2
00:00:05.947 --> 00:00:11.460
You plug in the coffee machine, set the fridge to four degrees, and line up twelve chairs.

3
00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:13.530
Nobody writes any of it down.

4
00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.515
A year later she opens a second café, exactly the same please.

5
00:00:17.515 --> 00:00:21.564
And you stand in the empty room, and can't remember half of it.

6
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:23.903
So we write it down.

7
00:00:23.903 --> 00:00:27.745
The first idea is a list of steps, a shell script.

8
00:00:27.745 --> 00:00:36.659
On a computer our café is one container, a sealed box running nginx, a program that serves a web page, on port 8090.

9
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:37.788
Run it once.

10
00:00:37.788 --> 00:00:38.478
It works.

11
00:00:38.478 --> 00:00:44.695
Run it again, and the second run fails, because the name tf-web is already taken.

12
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.902
A list of steps only knows how to start from nothing.

13
00:00:47.902 --> 00:00:49.998
It has no idea what is already there.

14
00:00:49.998 --> 00:00:55.267
Wouldn't it be nice to describe the finished room, and let a helper work out what is missing?

15
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.568
That helper is Terraform.

16
00:00:57.568 --> 00:01:04.263
Picture an architect's drawing of the house, and a builder who compares the drawing with the house on every visit.

17
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:07.418
The drawing is your Terraform files.

18
00:01:07.418 --> 00:01:14.059
They describe the desired state: one nginx container called tf-web, on port 8090.

19
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:18.789
The house is the real world, the containers Docker is actually running.

20
00:01:18.789 --> 00:01:20.445
Right now, there are none.

21
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:24.636
And the builder keeps a notebook of everything built so far.

22
00:01:24.636 --> 00:01:28.605
That notebook is the state, a file called terraform.tfstate.

23
00:01:28.605 --> 00:01:29.796
Today it is empty.

24
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.371
Every run, the builder lines up these three pictures, and writes down the difference as a list of actions.

25
00:01:36.371 --> 00:01:37.693
That list is the plan.

26
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.036
In the files, but not in the state?

27
00:01:41.036 --> 00:01:41.760
Create it.

28
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.427
The plan prints a plus.

29
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:46.174
You type terraform apply, and say yes.

30
00:01:46.174 --> 00:01:50.989
It also pulls the nginx image first, because the container refers to it.

31
00:01:50.989 --> 00:01:54.533
Then Docker starts the container, and gives it an ID.

32
00:01:54.767 --> 00:02:04.395
And the builder writes it in the notebook: this address, docker_container.web, is the container with ID 9aa5b095.

33
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.600
Now the question from the start.

34
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.538
You run apply again, without changing anything.

35
00:02:09.538 --> 00:02:11.788
Does the builder make a second café?

36
00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:14.960
Watch.

37
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.224
First, Terraform asks Docker about every ID in the notebook.

38
00:02:20.224 --> 00:02:23.470
Those are the Refreshing state lines.

39
00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.576
Is 9aa5b095 still there?

40
00:02:25.576 --> 00:02:27.331
Yes, with port 8090.

41
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:30.537
Files, notebook and world all agree.

42
00:02:30.537 --> 00:02:32.289
So the plan is empty.

43
00:02:32.289 --> 00:02:33.207
No changes.

44
00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:35.775
Now we can name what we just saw.

45
00:02:35.775 --> 00:02:39.796
Describing the result instead of the steps is called declarative.

46
00:02:39.796 --> 00:02:45.487
And a command that gives the same result however many times you run it is called idempotent.

47
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:52.549
The second rule of the plan: an object in both the files and the state, but with other settings?

48
00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:53.211
Change it.

49
00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:56.269
Change restart from no to unless-stopped.

50
00:02:56.269 --> 00:02:59.450
Docker can change that on a running container.

51
00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.424
So the plan shows a tilde: update in place.

52
00:03:02.424 --> 00:03:04.084
Same container, same ID.

53
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.246
So far so good.

54
00:03:06.246 --> 00:03:08.617
Now one more tiny edit.

55
00:03:08.617 --> 00:03:11.710
Change the port, 8090 to 8091.

56
00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.359
One word, again.

57
00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:14.803
Another tilde?

58
00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:17.676
No.

59
00:03:17.676 --> 00:03:21.379
Docker cannot move a running container to a new port.

60
00:03:21.379 --> 00:03:23.964
So the plan says minus plus: replace.

61
00:03:23.964 --> 00:03:26.619
Destroy the old one, create a new one.

62
00:03:26.619 --> 00:03:29.553
And the line ends with forces replacement.

63
00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:33.201
Apply, and the container comes back with a new ID.

64
00:03:33.201 --> 00:03:35.949
For nginx, that is a second of downtime.

65
00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:39.109
For a database, it can mean an empty database.

66
00:03:39.333 --> 00:03:39.809
Well...

67
00:03:39.809 --> 00:03:43.208
that was a lot of building for one changed number.

68
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:45.863
So learn the four symbols.

69
00:03:45.863 --> 00:03:46.878
Plus creates.

70
00:03:46.878 --> 00:03:48.674
Tilde updates in place.

71
00:03:48.674 --> 00:03:50.235
Minus plus replaces.

72
00:03:50.235 --> 00:03:52.187
And minus alone destroys.

73
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.491
And build one habit while the containers are toys.

74
00:03:55.491 --> 00:03:59.571
Read the summary line, and search the plan for forces replacement.

75
00:03:59.571 --> 00:04:02.724
If the numbers surprise you, stop and find out why.

76
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:05.426
One honest limit of the picture.

77
00:04:05.426 --> 00:04:07.780
This builder only comes when called.

78
00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:14.319
Terraform looks only when you run plan or apply, so a change made by hand stays until your next run.

79
00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:16.621
So the moral of the story.

80
00:04:16.621 --> 00:04:19.306
Write the finished room down, not the steps.

81
00:04:19.306 --> 00:04:25.106
Terraform compares your files, its notebook and the real world, and the difference is the plan.

82
00:04:25.106 --> 00:04:28.585
That is why running it twice gives you one café, not two.

83
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.201
A quick check.

84
00:04:30.201 --> 00:04:33.633
A teammate deletes the tf-web container by hand.

85
00:04:33.633 --> 00:04:36.492
You run plan, without changing any file.

86
00:04:36.492 --> 00:04:38.208
What does the plan show?

87
00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:41.453
A create.

88
00:04:41.453 --> 00:04:48.752
The refresh asks Docker about the ID in the notebook, Docker says it is gone, and the drawing still asks for it.
